## Project Log 3 — Feasibility Validation Framework

### 1. Primary Research Strategy

Our primary research will focus on college students and young adults who are actively interested in meeting new friends, expanding their social circles, or finding people to attend events with. We plan to survey approximately 40–50 students and conduct 8–10 deeper interviews, asking open-ended questions such as: “How do you currently meet new people outside of your existing friend group?”, “Tell us about the last time you wanted to attend an event but did not have anyone to go with,” “What makes meeting people through existing apps uncomfortable or difficult?”, and “What would make you feel comfortable meeting a new person or group in real life?” To reduce confirmation bias, we will ask participants about their current behaviors and past experiences before explaining our proposed solution, avoid questions like “Would you use our app?”, and actively document reasons people would not use a friend-matching platform. This will help us determine whether the underlying pain point is actually strong enough to support a dedicated product rather than simply validating features we already want to build.

### 2. Secondary Research Map

We will use research from Pew Research Center on friendship, social media, and how young adults form and maintain relationships to understand broader social behavior within our target demographic. We will also examine reports from Sensor Tower or data.ai on downloads, engagement, and growth within social networking and social discovery mobile apps to evaluate the health of the software market surrounding our product. In addition, we will analyze public information from platforms such as Bumble For Friends, Meetup, and Eventbrite to understand demand for friendship matching, group formation, and event-based social discovery. Together, these sources will help us estimate whether the market for digital social connection is growing and whether users are increasingly comfortable using software to turn online discovery into real-world relationships.

### 3. Fatal Flaw Filter

The greatest risk to Involve.me is the cold-start problem: the platform only becomes valuable if users can quickly find enough compatible people, groups, or events near them. Even if users like the idea, they may stop using the platform if there are too few relevant matches or if they do not trust strangers enough to turn an online match into an in-person interaction. During validation, we will test a prototype with a small group of target users and measure whether they actually browse matches, express interest in other users or groups, and indicate willingness to meet through an event. If fewer than roughly 30% of test users show meaningful interest in connecting with a suggested person or group after seeing realistic profiles and event options, we would reconsider the broad matching approach and potentially pivot toward a narrower model centered on existing events, communities, or verified student groups.
